\abstract{
We study admissions with one open category and several reserve categories when students may qualify for several reserve categories at once. Under one-to-one counting an admitted student fills at most one category, so a mechanism must decide both who is admitted and which category each overlapping student fills. We add a score constraint bounding the gap between the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff. Non-wastefulness must then be restated relative to this constraint, while within-category fairness need not be weakened. A student-proposing score-elevated deferred-acceptance mechanism (SPSE) is feasible, non-wasteful, strategy-proof and free of justified envy, but need not be score-feasible, and we isolate the role of overlap: routing a multiply eligible student can itself decide whether the cutoff rule survives. We then show that score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness may be jointly unattainable, through a Hall-type obstruction on non-singleton category sets, so any mechanism enforcing the cutoff rule must abandon one of them. Threshold-adjusting deferred acceptance (TA-DA) keeps the announced capacities and idles reserve seats; preference-aware overlapping de-reservation (PA-ODR) keeps every surviving seat filled and gives up fixed capacities, forfeiting strategy-proofness and no justified envy. A prototype on randomized instances prices the two against each other.
}

