\section{\uppercase{Introduction}}
Reserve-based admissions and controlled school choice are studied extensively \cite{Abdu05a,EHYY14a,HYY13a,EcYe15a,AyTu20a,AyTu23a}. In standard formulations each student belongs to a single protected category or none; in many real systems, however, categories overlap. S\"onmez and Yenmez study overlapping reserves together with the vertical--horizontal system of Indian affirmative action \cite{SoYe22a}; related overlapping-type environments appear in \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,DKT23a,CGH20a} and, more broadly, under distributional constraints in \cite{GKK+17a,KaKo24a}. Indian engineering admissions, the setting closest to ours, are described in \cite{BCC+19b}. That \emph{which} slot a student is counted against is itself a design variable is made explicit by Kominers and S\"onmez \cite{KoSo16a}, and the cost of getting that accounting wrong is documented in \cite{DKPS18a}. The order in which reserve categories are processed is likewise consequential rather than procedural, as Pathak, S\"onmez, \"Unver and Yenmez \cite{PSUY24a} and Pathak, Rees-Jones and S\"onmez \cite{PRS23a} show; the same sensitivity reappears here in Example~\ref{ex:order}.

Once categories overlap, a mechanism must decide not only which students are admitted but how overlapping students are counted, since under one-to-one counting an overlapping student fills at most one reserve slot. The category assigned to a student is therefore not a bookkeeping detail but part of the allocation. We add a second ingredient, a score constraint keeping the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff close. This is not a stylized device: in \emph{Ashoka Kumar Thakur vs.\ Union of India} the Supreme Court of India recommended a maximum ten-point difference between the open and OBC cutoffs, and Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} analyze that directive; cutoff-based concerns appear more generally in \cite{AyTu23a,ABB24a,HuZh25a}. Under such a rule a reserve category may be nominally available yet unusable, because every remaining eligible student would violate the bound.

These ingredients affect the standard desiderata \cite{EHYY14a,KHIY17a,GKK+17a} asymmetrically. Non-wastefulness must be restated because an empty reserve category may be unusable under the cutoff rule, while within-category fairness need not be: replacing a matched student by a higher-scoring eligible one can only raise that category's cutoff (Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}). With overlap, a high-score student eligible for several categories can protect the cutoff in only one of them, because she can be counted only once.

\subsubsection{Relation to the closest prior work.}
S\"onmez and Yenmez \cite{SoYe22a} take reserve capacities as given and impose no cutoff-gap requirement, the constraint driving every negative result here. Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} study exactly the score-elevated policy and the ten-point rule, but without overlapping eligibility, so their mechanism never decides \emph{which} category a student fills; their later hierarchical treatment of overlapping horizontal categories \cite{AyTu26a} does admit multiple qualifications, but in a one-to-all framework and with no cutoff-gap rule, so again no routing decision arises. We should be explicit about what is new. That a score-elevated mechanism can breach a cutoff-gap rule is \emph{not} new and needs no overlap; it is the inconsistency of \cite{AyTu23b}. What overlap adds is a second, independent cause: when a student is eligible for several categories but counted once, the routing decision itself decides whether the cutoff rule survives, and a routing optimal for her can destroy a score-feasibility another routing preserves. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} exhibits this cause, Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} separates it from the first, and Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} makes the same separation for the existence question by hypothesis. The de-reservation response is in the spirit of \cite{AyTu23a}.

\subsubsection{Contributions.}

% \textcolor{red}{Contribution needs to say: adapt the properties to this setting; use DA/SPSE and show it does not guarantee score-feasibility; state the impossibility result; make PA-ODR/de-reservation the main constructive contribution; include the small simulation/prototype check.}

\begin{enumerate}
\item We adapt the standard properties to overlapping reserves with a cutoff-gap rule: non-wastefulness must be score-relative, while within-category fairness remains unweakened (Section~\ref{sec:model}). The weakening is not idle bookkeeping: TA-DA below satisfies the score-relative notion and violates the plain one, in $46$ of $100$ randomized instances (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\item We modify student-proposing deferred acceptance for score-elevated categories (SPSE). SPSE is feasible, non-wasteful, strategy-proof and free of justified envy, but using DA this way does not guarantee score-feasibility (Section~\ref{sec:spse}).
\item We prove that score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness may be jointly unattainable, even when each reserve category is individually fulfillable under the cutoff rule (Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}, Section~\ref{sec:impossibility}). This is the pivot of the paper: a mechanism enforcing the cutoff rule must give up one of the three, and the last two sections are the two ways of doing so.
\item We study TA-DA, which keeps the announced capacities and gives up reserve-completeness by restricting proposals to score-admissible pairs. It is score-feasible and fair within categories, but only score-relatively non-wasteful, and it pays for the cutoff rule in coverage rather than in quota (Section~\ref{sec:tada}).
\item We propose PA-ODR, a deliberate de-reservation mechanism that gives up fixed capacities instead. It enforces score-feasibility and retains non-wastefulness relative to final capacities and within-category fairness, but gives up strategy-proofness and no justified envy (Section~\ref{sec:paodr}).
\item We implement the reserve-side choice as maximum-weight bipartite matching, formulate minimum de-reservation, and report a prototype comparing all three mechanisms across capacity regimes (Remark~\ref{rem:computation}, Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes}, Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{enumerate}

