This is not evidence of average-case performance; it prices the trade-off Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} forces. Four readings survive scrutiny.

First, SPSE breaches the cutoff rule in roughly half the instances of every family, so Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} is neither an artifact of three-student examples nor something scale washes out.

Second, the score-relative reading of non-wastefulness earns its keep. TA-DA satisfies Definition~\ref{def:nw} in all $300$ instances while violating the plain version in $54$, $52$ and $46$ of them: the idle seats of Example~\ref{ex:tada-idle} are common, not pathological. Neither SPSE nor PA-ODR ever separates the two readings, so without TA-DA in the picture the weakening of Definition~\ref{def:nw} would have no witness. The rates also line up with SPSE's breach rates almost exactly, which is no coincidence: TA-DA must idle a seat roughly when SPSE would have used it illegally.

Third, and this is what the capacity-poor families conceal, the two responses to Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} charge in different currencies. Where capacity binds, PA-ODR matches exactly as many students as SPSE and pays purely in quota, $39\%$ and $49\%$ of the reserve seats; TA-DA pays purely in coverage, $4.79$ and $15.25$ students, and keeps every announced seat. Where capacity is slack the gap widens sharply. PA-ODR then matches $56.61$ students, more than SPSE's $55.48$ and exactly the $\min\{\sum_c q^c,|I|\}$ bound it saturates by construction, so the coverage gain that Remark~\ref{sec:trigger} offers in defence of the reserve-completeness stopping rule is real. But it costs $94\%$ of the reserve seats to buy $1.13$ students, while TA-DA retires nothing and matches $46.83$. Whether that is a good bargain is a policy question and not one this paper should answer; what the model contributes is that the bargain must be struck.

Fourth, the price tracks $\delta$ rather than an indiscriminate appetite for de-reserving. On the first family, $\delta=2$ leaves SPSE score-feasible in $34$ instances and retires $30.32$ of the $47.99$ reserve seats, while $\delta=4,8,15,25$ give $43,54,79,97$ and $26.38,18.57,7.84,0.61$ respectively. At a permissive gap the constraint is close to slack and all three mechanisms nearly coincide.

Two caveats. Running times are medians on one laptop and are reported only to show that nothing here is intractable; TA-DA's cost is dominated by the $|I|$-fold threshold scan of Algorithm~\ref{alg:tada}, which a binary search over thresholds would likely reduce, and we make no claim of an optimized implementation. And the breach rates mix the two causes of Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause}: lowering the overlap probability to $0.15$ leaves SPSE score-feasible in only $6$ of $100$ instances of the first family, worse rather than better, because thin eligibility strands weak candidates in reserve categories. The empirical breach rate is therefore not a measure of the overlap-specific cause we isolate in Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf}.

\section{\uppercase{Conclusion}}

Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students count toward exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so non-wastefulness must be read relative to the constraint, and full capacity utilization can fail even when every quota is individually honourable. Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} makes that failure precise and forces a choice: score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness cannot be had together. SPSE declines the choice and gives up score-feasibility, keeping the incentive and no-envy properties of deferred acceptance. TA-DA gives up reserve-completeness, keeping every announced seat on the books and leaving some of them empty. PA-ODR gives up the announced capacities, retiring the seats it cannot fill. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} records what each keeps and Table~\ref{tab:prototype} what each costs.

We should not overstate what overlap explains: cutoff-gap breaches and order dependence can occur without it. The specifically overlapping content is the routing of multiply eligible students and the joint-versus-individual Hall obstruction of Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}, which Remark~\ref{rem:hall} identifies as the gap between Hall's condition on singleton category sets and on larger ones.

